  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper invokes published language-model data-scaling laws as motivation for its log-scale notation, but does not test those laws. Its empirical analysis relates language resource profiles to benchmark scores.",
    "gate_note": "The paper says no models were trained. Its benchmark regressions use language-resource components as predictors, not model parameter count, training tokens, compute, or pretraining loss. The scaling-law statement in Section 2 is cited background, not a result of this study. Table 2's regression results are recorded as related findings, not as tests of the claim versions.",
    "test_reason": "The paper trains no models. It cites Kaplan et al. (2020) and Bansal et al. (2022) only to motivate floor-of-log10 quantization in its notation, which it calls 'primarily a communication choice'. Its regressions predict fixed published benchmark scores (NLLB-200, XLM-R Large) across languages from per-language resource counts. They do not vary model parameters, tokens or compute, and they do not measure pretraining loss, so no version is tested. The reader's 'uses_without_test/T4' overstates the dependence: the finding is background motivation, not a tool producing the paper's results.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is accurate. The authors propose RAN as a communication notation whose components carry complementary information about published MT, NER and POS scores. Bmax beats Ben for cross-lingual transfer, and they treat the small-sample R² values as descriptive, not predictive.",
    "recipe": "none",
    "recipe_differences": "No scaling-law method is implemented or compared; the cited scaling-law finding is used only as motivation for order-of-magnitude notation.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported regression results vary by task and RAN predictor; the authors note the small per-task samples and English-centric benchmarks.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "For fixed published models, linear regressions on log-scale language-resource components give training R² of 0.52 (MT), 0.76 (NER) and 0.72 (POS) with S+M+Bmax. Bmax is the strongest single predictor for NER and POS. Speaker count alone has R² of 0.00 to 0.05. The authors call these values descriptive only, given n=9 to 17.",
        "proposed_claim": "Across languages, downstream performance of a fixed multilingual model tracks order-of-magnitude per-language corpus availability, especially the largest parallel corpus.",
